@*
    One card. The glyph is for the eye and hidden from the accessibility tree; the words
    beside it are for the ear (CardWords). A screen reader reading "7♦" says whatever its
    symbol table happens to say, which is not a card.

    🔥 This split is what lets the table lose its prose without losing it from the page
    (BUILD-PLAN P13.5): the face can shrink to a glyph and grow to a card, and the accessible
    name stays the whole sentence either way.

    ⚠️ A JOKER IS WRITTEN, NOT TYPED. CardText writes it 🃏, which a terminal renders as a glyph
    and a browser renders as a small colour photograph of a card — illegible at chip size in
    both themes, and worse the smaller it gets. So this client writes its own mark.

    🔥 It is the letters JKR, and getting there cost two drawings (BUILD-PLAN P13.5). A jester's
    cap in SVG was the obvious answer and it was wrong twice: three lobes above a band reads as
    a CROWN whether the lobes are pointed or rounded, and ♛ already means "won the round" two
    panels away on the same felt; tilting two of them down to hang like bells stopped it being a
    crown and made it an abstract blob at the twenty-odd pixels it is actually drawn at. A real
    joker card has the word printed on it, and three letters are legible at every size this
    client uses, take the card's own red or black from the same rule the rank does, and cannot
    be mistaken for anything. ⚠️ Judged by zooming into a screenshot at true size, which is the
    only way any of this was ever going to be settled.
*@

<span class="chip @Shade @(Big ? "big" : null)">
    <span class="face" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="rank @(Card.Rank is null ? "wild" : null)">@Code</span><span class="pip">@Pip</span>
    </span>
    @if (Token is not null)
    {
        <span class="token" aria-hidden="true">@Token</span>
    }
    <span class="said">@Said</span>
</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>The card to draw.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public Card Card { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// A non-colour marker drawn beside it — <c>DisplayTokens</c>' <c>($)</c> and the rest
    /// (§3.11 A2). Null for a card with nothing to say about it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Token { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the marker means, said in words for whoever is listening rather than looking.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TokenWords { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this is a card the player handles rather than one they are merely shown.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>A card being shown does not need to be a card</b> — a seat's top discard, a card in a
    /// line of the log and a meld laid down are all condensed to a chip, because the felt is
    /// worth more than they are. <b>A card you press is a card</b>: big enough to read across a
    /// desk and well past the 40–44 px a touch target wants (§3.11 B11, WCAG 2.5.8).
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Big { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The card's short face — <c>CardText</c>'s, except for the joker.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>The one place this client disagrees with <c>CardText</c>, and it disagrees on
    /// purpose.</b> <c>CardText.DisplayCode(null)</c> is 🃏, which is right for a terminal and
    /// is a colour photograph in a browser — see the note at the top of this file. The console
    /// keeps its joker; a browser needs its own, and <b>this is display, which is exactly what a
    /// front end is allowed to decide</b> (P13.1). Nothing about identity moves: a joker is
    /// still a rankless card and <c>CardWords</c> still says "the red joker".
    /// </remarks>
    private string Code => Card.Rank is null ? "JKR" : CardText.DisplayCode(Card.Rank);

    private string Pip => CardText.DisplaySuit(Card.Suit);

    /// <summary>
    /// The card's own red or black (RULES.md §2.2) — the card's colour, saying nothing else.
    /// The word is in <see cref="Said"/> either way, so nothing here is carried by colour.
    /// </summary>
    private string Shade => Card.Color == CardColor.Red ? "red" : "black";

    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>It ends in a full stop, and that is not fussiness</b> (P13.4). A card sits next to
    /// whatever else is said about it — the per-card cost in a hand, the rest of a line in the
    /// log — and without the stop a screen reader runs the two together into
    /// <em>"five of clubs melds nothing"</em>, which is a sentence that means the opposite of
    /// itself. Found by reading a button's accessible name out of the browser.
    /// </remarks>
    private string Said => TokenWords is null
        ? $"{CardWords.For(Card)}."
        : $"{CardWords.For(Card)}, {TokenWords}.";
}
